  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThursday: The surface cold front from Wednesday will clear the CWA \nwith lower dewpoint air filtering in from the north. The core of the \ncooler 850 mb temperatures will be confined to IL and IN though, \nwhich means highs will be cooler generally north of I-70, while \nsouth of I-70 expect high temperatures in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nFriday and Saturday: Surface high pressure will quickly move east \ntowards the Mid-Atlantic states with widespread southerly flow \nestablishing itself across the area. In the upper level of the \natmosphere, the southern stream jet will begin to amplify with a \nclosed upper level low over Texas. Simultaneously, Invest-92L in the \neastern Gulf of Mexico (near 100 percent chance of development) will \nbe steered northward towards the eastern flank of the upper level \nlow. This is where uncertainty in the forecast rapidly increases. \nSome guidance shows 92L moving north towards MO, while other \nguidance has 92L moving north and then northeast. The other source \nof uncertainty is a potent shortwave diving southeast near British \nColumbia and a tropical system in the eastern Pacific (as supported \nby the WPC Ensemble Sensitivity Analysis). This causes the IQR in \ntemperatures for St. Louis to be 15 to 20 degrees by Sunday and\nMonday. As such, wide ranging differences in the deterministic \nGFS/ ECMWF for Sunday and Monday can be expected over the next \ncouple of days (and resultant EPS/ GEPS/ and GEFS). The ESA \nindicates that the sensitivity starts on Friday, meaning \npotentially not until Friday will guidance come into better \nagreement.",
